{
  "dom_id": "step-q130348",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+8{y}^{\u2032}+16y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+8{y}^{\u2032}+16y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(0)=-2.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=-2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-4A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-4x}-4Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-4A+B=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=1$ gives $B=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-4x}+2x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$",
